new=r"""\emph{No supply in the four-matrix class.} Hypothesis class: the Poisson limit; the four matrices \(T^a=M^{a4}\); the quartic term plus one bilinear term, with Hessian \(L(A)_a=-\Box A_a+D_a(D_bA^b)-2s\mathcal J_{ab}A^b+\mu^2A_a\); the background gauge function \(D_aA^a\), the ghost operator \(-\Box\), and a regulator that is a function of \(\Box_X\); the matrix-index form constant, \(\eta_{ab}\). In this class every operator is a polynomial with constant coefficients in the \(D^a\) and \(J^{ab}=\{M^{ab},\cdot\}\), that is, an element of the enveloping algebra of \(\mathfrak{so}(4,1)'\), the Lie algebra generated by the \(D^a\) and \(J^{ab}\) (the subalgebra of \(\mathfrak{so}(4,2)\) on the indices \(0,\dots,4\); the generators \(M^{a5}\) and \(M^{45}\) are not in it). The operator \((y_aD^a)^2\) is not an element of that enveloping algebra: its coefficients are the non-constant functions \(y_a\), which would have to be supplied by the missing generators. Hence in this class none of the action, the bilinear term, the background gauge, the ghosts and the \(\Box_X\) regulator produces the term \((y_aD^a)^2\). The full Casimir \(\tfrac12\eta_{AC}\eta_{BD}J^{AB}J^{CD}\) of \(\mathfrak{so}(4,2)\) has base symbol \(-2\ell^2g^{-1}\) (checked numerically), but it lies outside the enveloping algebra of \(\mathfrak{so}(4,1)'\) and enters neither the Hessian nor the ghost operator. Extensions of the matrix set by \(y^a\) with a positive-norm contraction (eight matrices \(T^a,y^a\); seven matrices \(T^a,y^i\)) have rank-six horizontal span, but the equation of motion then needs different bilinear coefficients for different matrices (checked by exact Poisson algebra). The eight-matrix set \(T^a,y^a\) with the \(y\)-block contracted by \(-\eta\) is stationary for one coefficient \(\mu^2=-4\), with horizontal rank six, and is not examined further.

"""
