i=t.index(r"\emph{Consequences.}")
t=t[:i]+r"""\emph{Consequences.} (i) In the matrix sets examined (\(T^a\); \(M^{Ac_0}\); the extended sets listed above), the term \((y_aD^a)^2\) is supplied by the matrix data only if the matrices are the position functions \(X^A=M^{A5}\); the price is that the horizontal metric induced by the contraction is then the Euclidean metric \(g\), and the conformally flat Lorentzian \(K\) of the four-matrix class is not obtained, so the level-one and stress results of the four-matrix class are not obtained in this presentation. (ii) For the hyperboloid, the vector zero modes of the leading symbol are lifted by \(+\mu^2\) only if the vanishing of the Casimir combination, which is exact on polynomial vector fields and on the compact form, continues to the normalisable sector; this continuation is not shown, and the negative formal eigenvalues \(-8,-4,-2,+2\) of the same operator on degree-one polynomials, like the negative modes of the compact background, may persist on normalisable states. (iii) Neither the sign nor the size of \(w\) follows: the weight-two coefficient and its variation under \(A\) are not computed, and the passage from a Euclidean coefficient to the Lorentzian one is a Wick-rotation hypothesis tied to Problem~E.

This is a statement for the Poisson limit, the leading symbol, equiregular points, the bosonic content and the single bilinear term; the self-duality of the flux is verified numerically and not proved, and the finite-size spectrum is for the compact form. It does not treat fermions, Moyal terms for the hyperboloid, or the Lorentzian emergent metric. It does not identify \(g\) with the emergent metric of Chapter~\ref{ch:vierbein-emergence}, does not derive Lorentzian signature, and leaves Assumption~\ref{ass:lorentzian} in place (Problem~E). This is a scope statement and not a retraction of any earlier result. This changes no ledger label; Problem~F remains open.
"""
